  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Hello,\n\nI would like to report an issue regarding the use of public group instances in VRChat. Unfortunately, users are unable to effectively moderate these instances and some administrators of large communities sometimes misuse this feature to ban individuals at their discretion, leading to the division of communities.\n\nPeople who are banned from these group instances are often individuals who have fallen out of favor with the administrators, resulting in situations where everyone else is in a public group instance, including the friends of the banned individual who cannot join due to their ban (what's more: when you try to join, you just get an almost incomprehensible error, and we don't even have a clear indication: you've been banned from the group).\n\nMoreover, there are some worlds where administrators abuse the system by instructing users to create public group instances under the pretext of \"protection\", when in reality, this is done solely to prevent banned users from participating. This misuse of the system undermines the integrity of public group instances.\n\nWhile the concept of public group instances is commendable and that some administrators need this feature to ban only unsavory users, some administrators seem to struggle with proper usage and occasionally abuse their power.\n\nTo address this, there are two potential solutions to me:\n\n- Remove Public Instances: VRChat could consider eliminating public group instances altogether to prevent these issues and maintain a more controlled environment.\n\n- Restrict Ban Effects: Alternatively, VRChat could modify the system to prevent banned individuals from accessing private group instances but allow them to participate in public instances. This would align better with the intended purpose of public instances and reduce the potential for abuse by administrators.\n\nI believe that implementing one of these solutions would greatly improve the VRChat experience for its users and prevent the misuse of this valuable feature.\n\nThank you for considering this proposal.",
